I reviewed the current main branch of [MEP-EA-01](https://github.com/madmonkeyru/MEP-EA-01?utm_source=chatgpt.com) against the MVP consolidation material and the authoritative UI/UX design system.

My conclusion: there are meaningful UI/UX opportunities, but they are mostly a consequence of the MVP becoming much more capable—not evidence that the underlying design system is weak.

The project has crossed an important threshold: UX should now shift from “feature navigation” toward “guided learning journey.”

1. The biggest opportunity: unify the learner journey

The implementation now supports substantially more than a conventional simulator:

Diagnostic → Results → Learn → Practice → Mastery → Simulation → Remediation → Retest

The problem is that this creates a new UX requirement: the student should not have to understand that architecture.

They should experience something closer to:

> “Aquí estás → esto es lo que necesitas mejorar → esto es lo que te recomiendo hacer → empieza.”



I would make the primary home/dashboard look approximately like:

BXM Matemáticas

> Tu preparación

Has trabajado 3 de 5 habilidades recomendadas.

Siguiente paso

Practicar: Funciones

Tu dominio actual: 54%
Último intento: 48%

[Practicar ahora]



Then secondary information:

- 📚 Continue learning
- 🎯 Your mastery
- 📝 Last simulation
- 🔁 Recommended remediation

This is more important than adding another dashboard chart.


---

2. Results should become a launchpad, not a report

The new diagnostic/progress architecture creates an opportunity to completely rethink results screens.

A traditional result:

> 32/60 correct
Score: 53%
Review answers



is relatively passive.

The MVP can now answer why the student performed that way and what to do next.

Instead:

Tu diagnóstico

Resultado general

53%

Lo que ya dominas

✓ Geometría básica
✓ Medidas
✓ Estadística descriptiva

Necesitas reforzar

⚠ Funciones
⚠ Relaciones algebraicas

Tu siguiente paso

> Empieza por Funciones — evidencia A2.x



[Aprender este tema]

That directly exploits the canonical evidence model being consolidated.


---

3. The Digital Temario should become the central learning surface

This is probably the highest-value UX opportunity after the dashboard.

The architecture already supports concepts, explanations, worked examples, visuals, micro-quizzes and practice.

Don't expose those as separate features.

Make them feel like one lesson.

For example:

Circunferencia

Lo esencial

Short explanation.

Visual

Interactive SVG.

Ejemplo

Worked calculation.

Comprueba

One micro-question.

Tu resultado

✓ Correcto

> Ahora prueba una variación del problema.



[Practicar esta habilidad]

This creates a natural progression:

understand → try → fail/succeed → practice → master

rather than:

read → leave lesson → find practice → select topic → start.


---

4. Add persistent "Why am I here?" context

Once diagnostic, evidence, lessons and practice are connected, the UI can continuously show context.

For example, at the top of a practice session:

> Practicas porque tu diagnóstico detectó una oportunidad en Funciones.



And during a lesson:

> Esta lección fortalece la habilidad A2.x.



After completion:

> Has completado esta habilidad. Próximo paso: demostrar dominio.



This small piece of UX could substantially increase perceived intelligence of the product.

It makes the system feel personalized without requiring an AI tutor.


---

5. Practice needs a stronger feedback loop

The implementation's assessment architecture is now sophisticated enough that practice shouldn't feel like a generic quiz.

After an incorrect response, don't merely say:

> ❌ Incorrecto



Use the existing pedagogical data:

> Incorrecto

Tu respuesta: B
Respuesta correcta: D

Qué ocurrió

Aquí confundiste el radio con el diámetro.

Recuerda

d = 2r

[Intentar una variación]



Then generate the next isomorphic item when appropriate.

This is especially valuable because the project has deliberately invested in evidence, provenance, difficulty and generated practice.

The UX should make that intelligence visible.


---

6. Make "mastery" visually understandable

One UX risk with an evidence-based architecture is exposing too much internal terminology.

Students don't necessarily need:

> Evidence ID
Cognitive level
familyId
provenance
canonical taxonomy



They need:

> Dominio de esta habilidad: 72%



and perhaps:

> 🟢 Dominada
🟡 En progreso
🔴 Necesita práctica



The underlying canonical evidence model should remain sophisticated.

The presentation should remain human.


---

7. Reduce cognitive load around simulation

The distinction between the official/reference 60-question benchmark and authored formative mocks is architecturally correct.

UX needs to make that distinction equally obvious.

I would explicitly label them:

Simulación oficial de referencia

60 preguntas · 150 min

> Basada en el formato de referencia oficial.
Úsala para medir tu preparación.



[Comenzar]

versus:

Simulacro de práctica

30 preguntas · ~60 min

> Diseñado para practicar y detectar oportunidades de mejora.



[Practicar]

That avoids the potentially confusing situation where two experiences both look like "exams."


---

8. Introduce a "study session" concept

This is a UX improvement I would seriously consider.

Instead of making students decide between:

- Lesson
- Practice
- Mastery
- Simulation
- Remediation

offer:

> ¿Qué quieres hacer hoy?

15 min — Reforzar una habilidad
30 min — Sesión de práctica
60 min — Prepararte con un simulacro



The recommendation engine chooses the underlying activities.

This is particularly appropriate for the adult-learner positioning in the design system: the product should accommodate students studying in short sessions after work.

The architecture already supports the necessary activities; this is primarily a presentation/orchestration problem.


---

9. Mobile UX should prioritize the next action

The current design specification already calls for 44×44px touch targets, responsive grids and sticky test navigation.

I would go one step further for the learning MVP.

On mobile, the bottom action should almost always answer:

> ¿Qué hago ahora?



Examples:

Lesson

[Continuar]

Correct answer

[Siguiente pregunta]

Incorrect answer

[Intentar otra]

Mastery achieved

[Continuar con la siguiente habilidad]

Simulation complete

[Ver mi plan de remediación]

Avoid making the learner scroll back through a long page to find the next action.


---

10. The top navigation probably needs simplification

As functionality has expanded, there is a temptation to expose everything:

> Diagnóstico | Temario | Práctica | Simulaciones | Progreso | Recursos | ...



I would instead consider a smaller conceptual navigation:

Inicio · Aprender · Practicar · Simular

with Progreso accessible from the learner profile/home.

Why?

Because these four labels correspond to student intent, rather than internal system modules.


---

11. Make official MEP resources feel integrated

This is another opportunity resulting from the current architecture.

The project explicitly positions official resources as part of the product.

Instead of a generic:

> Recursos oficiales



make them contextual.

While studying:

> Fuente oficial

Consulta la especificación correspondiente a esta habilidad.

[Ver recurso MEP]



That gives the product an important differentiator:

the learning experience isn't detached from the official source material.

It also makes the provenance architecture visible in a useful way.


---

12. Free vs Premium needs to be quieter

I would not put a paywall in the middle of the learning journey every time a premium capability exists.

Instead, let the student experience the value first.

For example:

> Has completado esta práctica.

Tu siguiente recomendación requiere acceso al plan completo.

[Ver qué incluye Premium]



The product should communicate:

> Premium gives you more preparation.



not:

> You clicked the wrong button.



This is particularly important now that the MVP has enough functionality to demonstrate real value before monetization.


---

13. Dark mode is good—but don't let it dominate the learning UI

The repository's design system has a coherent warm educational aesthetic:

- cream / warm surfaces
- stone neutrals
- indigo primary
- amber semantic accent
- Outfit headings
- Plus Jakarta Sans body

That's a good foundation.

The opportunity is to make the semantic hierarchy stronger than the decorative hierarchy.

For example:

Indigo = action

Amber = attention

Green = mastery

Red = error

Neutral = information

That allows students to understand screens quickly without reading everything.


---

14. Add "confidence" to important transitions

Another subtle opportunity from the new architecture:

When the system makes a recommendation, explain it briefly.

Instead of:

> Practicar funciones



use:

> Recomendado para ti

Tu diagnóstico mostró dificultades en funciones.

[Practicar ahora]



This turns an apparently arbitrary recommendation into a trustworthy one.

No AI explanation is required—the recommendation engine already has the evidence.


---

15. The four existing lessons should become the UX gold standard

I strongly agree with the consolidation strategy of not immediately expanding content everywhere.

Instead:

Take the four existing lessons and make them excellent.

They should establish the canonical experience for:

- mathematical typography
- KaTeX
- interactive visual
- explanation
- worked example
- micro-quiz
- feedback
- evidence association
- progress
- practice transition
- mobile layout

Then future lessons inherit that pattern.

This is a much better UX strategy than generating dozens of mediocre lesson screens.


